  "tags": [{"name": "phase-3"}, {"name": "sprint-10"}, {"name": "feature-b-refund"}],
  "meta": {
    "description": "Sprint 10 — I-1: Next.js checkout route calls this webhook with session_grant_id. The workflow creates the Stripe Checkout Session (metadata session_grant_id, session_id, student_id), POSTs session_grant_checkout_created to the n8n webhook (so the session_grants.stripe_session_id is persisted), POSTs the checkout-started email to /api/n8n/notify, and returns the checkout URL to Next.js. Sprint TASK 3 / Feature C: when the inbound body.kind == 'monthly', the Switch node routes to a separate Stripe Subscription-mode Checkout node (mode=subscription, line_items[0][price] from $env.STRIPE_PRICE_SUBSCRIPTION) for the Monthly Support product. TASK 15 / Feature B: when the inbound body.kind == 'pack_refund', a second Switch routes to a Stripe /v1/refunds HttpRequest node (mode=POST, Idempotency-Key=$json.body.refund_request_id, amount=$json.body.amount_cents, charge / payment_intent from the inbound body, metadata.refund_request_id for tracing). The Stripe refund is the authoritative money movement; Stripe `charge.refunded` is the inbound webhook that confirms the refund in the application DB. Both branches share the dead-letter safety net. Documented in n8n/docs/WORKFLOWS.md §2.1."
  }
